samples.guided_completion:
One simple way to do this in MATLAB is to use the `fimplicit` function to plot the implicit function defining the surface, and then add lines (vectors) to represent the ruling. Here is a code snippet that does this:

```matlab
% Define the variables
x = linspace(0, 10, 100);
z = x .* (x * 10); % Assuming z = x * sqrt(x)

% Plot the surface
surf(x, z, sqrt(z)); 
xlabel('x');
ylabel('sqrt(z)');

% Plot the ruling
n = 10; % Number of lines
for i = 1:n
    t = linspace(0, 2*pi, 100);
    x_line = x + cos(t(i)) * (10 - x) / 2;
    z_line = z + sin(t(i)) * (sqrt(z) - 1) / 2;
    plot3(x_line, z_line, sqrt(z_line), 'LineWidth', 2);
end
```

This code first plots the surface defined by `y = sqrt(xz)` (which is the upper part of the cone), and then plots 10 lines (vectors) that represent the ruling of the cone

